1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 Now, let's take a look at the third option, which is Amazon ECS in AWS.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,200
 There are two main container services, one is ECS, which comes in two variants,

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:17,400
 Fargate, which is serverless, and then ECS on EC2, where you provision and easy to instance.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,000
 And this is an AWS native service.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,200
 And then you also have the managed EKS.

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,800
 But the exam is mainly going to focus on Amazon ECS because it's a AWS native server.

7
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:35,800
 Now, in terms of the architecture for Amazon ECS or Elastic Container service,

8
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:41,800
 so it's a fully managed, highly scalable container orchestration service.

9
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:46,800
 So now in terms of the management, AWS Fargate is a serverless service,

10
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:50,800
 so you don't need to manage any servers.

11
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:56,800
 Everything related to scaling, monitoring will be taken care of by AWS.

12
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,800
 And then you also have the option of running the ECS on EC2 instance,

13
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:04,800
 which will be a customer managed instance.

14
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:09,800
 Now, in terms of the scaling, AWS Fargate can scale automatically.

15
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:16,800
 And for once running on Amazon EC2, you can set up some metrics, which allows it to scale.

16
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:24,800
 Now, the important thing to remember here is containers can scale in most cases much faster than EC2 instances.

17
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:29,800
 Now, in terms of pricing model, again, Fargate is completely serverless,

18
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,800
 so it's only based on pay as you use.

19
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:41,800
 And in terms of when you use it, it will be based on the VCPU and memory used per second.

20
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:50,800
 In case of the EC2 ECS, it will be based on the EC2 instance provision for the ECS cluster.

21
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:54,800
 Okay, now the important thing, execution limit.

22
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:58,800
 It is suitable for long running genii inference.

23
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:06,800
 And there is no hard execution limit unlike the AWS Lambda function, which is 15 minute limit.

24
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:11,800
 So, this is an important distinction in the exam to determine which service to use.

25
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:14,800
 If it is less than 15 minutes, you go with AWS Lambda.

26
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:18,800
 If it is more than 15 minutes, then you have the option of ECS or EC2.

27
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:22,800
 Now, some of the core use cases for ECS.

28
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,800
 First is the custom Genia orchestration.

29
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:31,800
 So, if you have to write your orchestration logic, you can write the code for that and run it on ECS.

30
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:33,800
 The second one is the rack pipelines.

31
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,800
 I know we have not discussed a rack pipelines.

32
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:41,800
 And there are various steps involved in the rack pipeline, chunking, vector data bases, the retrieval and so on.

33
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:46,800
 And in those scenarios, and let's say you are using some open source frameworks,

34
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,800
 such as Lang Jane or open source databases.

35
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:56,800
 So, to execute those steps, you will need to write some code and that code can run on ECS.

36
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:01,800
 Then microsaves based architecture and in the exam, if you hear anything related to,

37
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:05,800
 then the most likely service is ECS.

38
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:13,800
 It can also be used for model hosting and we saw in EC2 instance how EC2 can be used for hosting the model.

39
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:23,800
 ECS also provides you option for model hosting where you can use GPU based inferences using ECS on EC2.

40
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:28,800
 So, you have G5 and P4D etc that can be used.

41
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:33,800
 And then finally, it can be used for any long running inference workloads.

42
